# Data from the Breeding Bird Survey, subsetted to Florida.
  
  * Breeding Bird Survey <https://doi.org/10.5066/P97WAZE5>, 
  * BBS Assistant R package at <https://github.com/trashbirdecology/bbsAssistant>
  
# In addition to the BBS data, I've added some spatial data:
  * Mean annual temperature
  * Mean annual precipitation
  * Fraction built
  * Human footprint

# Two things to know before modelling with this file

  * **There are no zeros.** A row exists only where a species was detected, so
    `total_sightings` runs from 1 upward and absences are missing rather than
    recorded as 0. Counts taken straight from this file are therefore counts
    *given presence*, which biases means upward and flattens any effect that
    works by making a species absent. To get the zeros, build the full
    route-by-year grid yourself: the routes surveyed in a year are
    `unique(Route[Year == y])`, and any of them missing from a species' rows
    held none of that species.
  * **The spatial columns do not change over time.** `frac_built`, `footprint`,
    `mean_annual_temp`, `mean_annual_precip`, `Latitude` and `Longitude` are each
    constant within a route across all 24 years: they describe where a route is,
    not what happened on it. They can explain spatial variation, and cannot
    explain a trend through time.

  79,991 rows: 112 routes, 1996 to 2019. 1996 has a single route in it.
